// BIP-39: a secret key written as words a person can copy down.
//
// A 256-bit key is 64 hex characters, and a human transcribing that gets it
// wrong. Twenty-four words from a fixed list of 2048 carry the same
// entropy, survive bad handwriting, and — because the last word encodes a
// checksum — say so when one of them is wrong instead of silently
// unlocking a different wallet.
//
// ## The list is the format
//
// A word's *index* is eleven bits of the key. So the list cannot be
// sorted, extended or translated word-for-word: a mnemonic is only
// meaningful beside the exact list it was made from. The English list is
// here; the others are the same idea with different words and the same
// indices.
//
// ## The checksum is small on purpose
//
// It is the first ENT/32 bits of the SHA-256 of the entropy — four bits for
// a twelve-word phrase. That catches an accidental mistake and is not meant
// to stop somebody constructing a valid phrase, which is trivial. It is a
// typo check, not a security boundary.
//
// ## The passphrase does not have a wrong answer
//
// Any passphrase produces a valid seed, and a different one produces a
// different wallet. There is no way to tell a mistyped passphrase from a
// deliberate second wallet, which is the design: it is what makes the
// feature deniable, and it is why a mistyped one looks like an empty
// account rather than an error.
import crypto_hash
import sha512
import unicodenorm

type MnemonicError =
    | BadEntropyLength(bits: i64)
    | BadWordCount(count: i64)
    | UnknownWord(word: str)
    | BadChecksum()

/// The BIP-39 English wordlist, in its published order: the index of
/// a word *is* eleven bits of the entropy, so the order is the format
/// and not a convenience. Every word is unique in its first four
/// letters, which is what makes a mnemonic recoverable from a
/// half-legible piece of paper.
